WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.210
Pinocchio is a software
system for automatic animation

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:04.440
of 3D characters.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:12.060
Given a 3D character mesh and
a generic skeletal motion,

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.460
Pinocchio adapts the
skeleton to the character

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:15.990
and applies the
motion to the shape.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.745
The result is an
animated character.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:32.220
As we developed
Pinocchio, we used

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:36.520
about 70 different
characters for testing.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.260
After Pinocchio
had been completed,

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.750
we tested it on 16
biped characters

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:43.490
that we did not see or
use during development.

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:46.930
Here, all 16 of our test
characters are shown.

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:50.290
A single biped skeleton was
used to animate all of them,

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:52.435
demonstrating the
generality of our approach.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.640
Three of these characters
required a single manual hint

00:00:58.640 --> 00:00:59.880
to produce a good motion.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.790
On a mid-range PC,
Pinocchio typically

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.463
takes under a minute
to animate a character.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.000
Pinocchio can produce
plausible animations,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.590
even in some unexpected cases.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:36.040
It can also work with
non-biped skeletons.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.190
We were unable to obtain a
centaur for motion capture.

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:41.710
This motion is the
capture of a human hopping

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:43.015
applied to a centaur skeleton.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.590
Pinocchio works as follows.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.620
It packs spheres
into the character

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:56.410
and constructs a graph
on their centers.

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:58.720
It then finds an embedding
of the given skeleton

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.740
into this graph that minimizes
a measure of distortion.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.320
It uses continuous
optimization to refine

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:09.270
the positions of the skeleton
joints within the character.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.460
It solves the diffusion
equilibrium equation

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.250
on the character surface
to compute the bone weights

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.260
for skeleton
subspace deformation.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:22.790
Once the character
is rigged, Pinocchio

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:26.030
uses online motion retargeting
to eliminate foot skate.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:32.570
Pinocchio requires
that the character

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:34.640
be given in roughly
the same orientation

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.880
and pose as the skeleton.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.280
This is the result if the
character's orientation

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:42.690
is flipped vertically.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.120
Pinocchio has no
capability to differentiate

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.470
between materials.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.410
This leads to unrealistic,
rubbery motion of a knight's

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:50.715
armor and a woman's dress.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.110
In our blind validation
test, Pinocchio correctly

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:01.010
embedded the skeleton into
13 of our 16 test models.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:03.050
Pinocchio incorrectly
embedded the skeleton

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:05.900
into these three
test characters.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.750
However, a manual hint for
the placement of one joint

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:11.810
was sufficient to correct
the problem for each of them.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:14.150
The asymmetry in the
middle character's skeleton

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:17.540
is due to the asymmetry
in the character.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:19.790
The following animations
demonstrate a results

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:22.270
on the more interesting of
our test characters close up.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.860
Pinocchio is best suited for
children and amateur animators.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:09.070
It generates pleasing animations
automatically in almost all

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:12.510
of our tests with
unfamiliar 3D shapes.

